# Runtime data
#
# observations/ is NOT blanket-ignored: fetch-2026-09-08.jsonl is a deliberate,
# tracked work artifact (reference data for the fetch-chain, committed in
# dd39b29). Ignoring the directory while the file stays in the index leaves the
# rule and reality contradicting each other, so the log is excluded from the
# ignore patterns instead. New runtime logs remain untracked.
observations/*
!observations/fetch-2026-09-08.jsonl
*.jsonl
!observations/fetch-2026-09-08.jsonl
cve_cache/cve-feed.json
runs/
*.pid

# Recon scan output (auto-generated)
*_all.txt
*_subs.txt
*_urls.txt

# Engagement runtime data (per-target recon, results, chain state)
# Only .md documents are tracked under engagements/ — anything generated at
# runtime can carry target data (origin IP, WAF product, findings) and must
# never be committed. Adding the rule BEFORE any writer exists means a first
# test write can never reach the index.
#
# Ignore engagement data by *extension at any depth* rather than by ignoring
# the target directory. Ignoring the directory first (engagements/*/) makes the
# re-include rules below dead: git never descends into an excluded directory, so
# no '!engagements/<target>/doc.md' can ever match and a new engagement's
# documents silently become uncommittable. Denying data files at depth, then
# re-including documents, achieves the same protection without that trap.
engagements/**/*.json
engagements/**/*.csv
engagements/**/*.har
engagements/**/*.html
engagements/**/*.txt
engagements/**/*.log
engagements/**/*.lock
engagements/**/*.tmp
engagements/**/evidence/
# Re-include tracked documents last, so the rules above cannot re-ignore them.
!engagements/*.md
!engagements/*/*.md
!engagements/*/*/*.md
